 2. Part 2: What you can do 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Jim writes: 

 I spell this out not to scare you or cause you to throw up your hands and retreat from reality.

 You can only make wise decisions for your career, your life and your family if you're living in the future — understanding what's coming, and living these historic times with your head in the game. Axios' expert journalists are working hour by hour to be your clinical, useful guide to this head-spinning era. Three very practical steps: 

 👀 Be aware: This is an exciting time to be alive. So learn about AI — the good, bad and ugly. And think more deeply about what you expect from your employer and elected representatives. 🔒 Be vigilant: This past week's Axios reporting about AI agents showed the importance of locking down your passwords. Store every account in a secure password manager and use a unique password for each one. Use passkeys where offered. Protect your email and phone with an authenticator app, since whoever controls those can reset everything else. And don't hand a password or login code to anyone who contacts you, human or AI agent. 🔭 Be ready: This past week also showed how AI is the market. So if you have a 401(k) or other investments, make sure to understand how concentrated you are in different sectors. Seek out smart diversification strategies. And find a few news sources, like Axios, that you can trust to make sense of all the craziness. Share this column ... Let us know what you think: finishline@axios.com . 

 📈 If you're a CEO or on a CEO's team: Ask to join Jim's new weekly Axios C-Suite newsletter. 
 
