 8. ⚾ 1 fast thing: Brew Crew pitcher sets record 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Jacob Misiorowski of the Milwaukee Brewers strikes out Victor Mesa Jr. of the Tampa Bay Rays in the second inning of Game 1. Photo: Daniel Shirey/MLB Photos via Getty Images The very first pitch of this year's World Series made history. 

 Milwaukee Brewers pitcher Jacob "Miz" Misiorowski threw a 103.5 mph fastball against the Tampa Bay Rays on Friday — setting a new record for the fastest World Series pitch. The Chicago Cubs' Aroldis Chapman held the previous mark for the fastest Series pitch: 103.2 mph in the ninth inning of Game 2 in 2016. 🍻 The relentless Brew Crew was down four runs last night but stunned the Rays with a 5-4 comeback for a 2-0 World Series lead.

 The Rays host the Brewers in Tampa Bay for Game 3 tomorrow at 8 p.m. ET. More on the record ... More on last night ... More on Miz . 

 
 
